---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import SystemPromptBuilder from '../../components/SystemPromptBuilder.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="System Prompt Template Builder" description="Fill in role, tone, guidelines, and format to generate a structured system prompt.">
  <SystemPromptBuilder client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why structure beats a single paragraph</h2>
  <p>A system prompt written as one dense paragraph is harder for both the model and future editors to parse — separating role, tone, guidelines, and format into distinct sections makes the prompt clearer to follow and much easier to update one piece at a time later.</p>

  <h2>How to use this builder</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Fill in the assistant's role, desired tone, specific guidelines, and output format.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated structured system prompt.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Writing vague guidelines</strong> like "be helpful" that don't give the model concrete, actionable direction for your specific use case.</li>
    <li><strong>Overloading the prompt with too many rules</strong>, making it harder for the model to prioritize which instructions matter most.</li>
    <li><strong>Not iterating based on actual output</strong> — a system prompt should be refined against real model responses, not written once and assumed correct.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Setting up a new AI assistant's behavior for a specific application</li>
    <li>Structuring an existing unwieldy system prompt into clearer sections</li>
    <li>Standardizing system prompt format across a team building multiple AI features</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>How long should a system prompt be?</strong> As long as needed to convey necessary context and constraints clearly, but avoid padding — every additional instruction adds tokens and potential for the model to deprioritize what matters most.</p>
</ToolLayout>
